Based on your earlier setup (Express microservice on port 4500, DeepL API v2, Redis cache, chatbot backend on 5000), these are the changes that matter most for speed.

## 1. Cache first, with a proper key and TTL
Your 85% hit rate is good, so the remaining latency is mostly the 15% of misses. Hash the key, normalize the text, and use the promise-based Redis client. Your earlier snippet mixed callbacks with `await`, which won't run.

## 2. Reuse connections (keep-alive)
Creating a new TLS connection per request adds tens of ms. Reuse one HTTP agent.

## 3. Batch and deduplicate
DeepL accepts an array of `text` values in one call. Also coalesce identical in-flight requests so concurrent duplicates trigger one API call.

## 4. Set a timeout and fall back to the original text
This keeps the chatbot responsive when DeepL is slow or down.

## 5. Use header auth
Passing `auth_key` as a query param is deprecated. Use the `Authorization` header.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const axios = require('axios');
const https = require('https');
const crypto = require('crypto');
const { createClient } = require('redis');

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const redis = createClient();
redis.connect();

const deepl = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://api.deepl.com/v2',
  timeout: 2000, // fail fast
  headers: { Authorization: `DeepL-Auth-Key ${process.env.DEEPL_KEY}` },
  httpsAgent: new https.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 50 }),
});

const inflight = new Map();
const keyFor = (text, lang) =>
  'tr:' + lang + ':' + crypto.createHash('sha1').update(text.trim()).digest('hex');

async function translate(text, targetLang) {
  const key = keyFor(text, targetLang);

  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return cached;

  if (inflight.has(key)) return inflight.get(key); // dedupe concurrent calls

  const p = deepl
    .post('/translate', { text: [text], target_lang: targetLang })
    .then(async (res) => {
      const out = res.data.translations[0].text;
      await redis.set(key, out, { EX: 60 * 60 * 24 }); // 24h TTL
      return out;
    })
    .finally(() => inflight.delete(key));

  inflight.set(key, p);
  return p;
}

app.post('/translate', async (req, res) => {
  const { text, targetLang } = req.body;
  try {
    res.json({ translated_text: await translate(text, targetLang) });
  } catch (err) {
    // fallback so the chatbot never breaks
    res.json({ translated_text: text, fallback: true });
  }
});

app.listen(4500);
```

## Further steps if you need more speed
- **Batch multi-message requests:** send an array of texts in one DeepL call, using `redis.mGet` to check the cache for all of them first.
- **Retries:** add one or two retries with exponential backoff, but only for 429/5xx errors, and keep the total time under your latency budget.
- **Pre-warm the cache:** translate common phrases (greetings, UI strings) ahead of time.
- **Measure p95/p99, not just the average:** a 180ms average can hide slow misses. Log cache hits and misses separately to see where the time goes.
- **Co-locate services:** keep Redis and the microservice on the same host or network to avoid extra round trips.

